  <h2 class="mt-12">Season by Season</h2>
  <h3>Spring (March-May)</h3>
  <p>The best season. Temperatures range from 15-25 degrees C. March can be rainy, but by April the weather settles. The countryside is lush green, wildflowers bloom, and the Li River water levels are ideal for cruises. May has perfect weather but slightly more domestic tourists around the Labor Day holiday (first week of May).</p>

  <h3>Summer (June-August)</h3>
  <p>Hot and humid — temperatures regularly hit 32-35 degrees C. Afternoon thunderstorms are common but usually pass quickly. The landscape is at its greenest. Fewer Western tourists (summer is domestic travel season), so some hotels offer lower rates. If you can handle the heat, summer can be rewarding — the mist rising off the karst peaks after rain is spectacular.</p>

  <h3>Autumn (September-November)</h3>
  <p>The second-best season. September and October bring cooler temperatures (18-28 degrees C) and clear skies — the best light for photography. <strong>But avoid October 1-7</strong>: Golden Week is China's national holiday. Hotels triple in price, attractions are packed, and the Li River cruise sells out days ahead. November is quieter and still pleasant, though evenings get cool.</p>

  <h3>Winter (December-February)</h3>
  <p>Cold and misty — temperatures drop to 5-12 degrees C. Fewer tourists mean lower prices and empty viewpoints. The misty winter landscape has its own beauty (classic Chinese ink painting vibes), but you'll need warm clothes and should expect some rainy days. Hotels in Yangshuo can be chilly — check that your accommodation has proper heating.</p>

  <div class="mt-12 rounded-2xl border border-stone-200 bg-stone-50 p-6">
