  <h2 class="mt-12">Chinese Holidays to Avoid</h2>
  <p>Chinese holiday periods dramatically affect prices, crowds, and availability. Plan around these unless you specifically want to experience a festival:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Chinese New Year (late Jan–mid Feb):</strong> The biggest holiday. Everything is packed, domestic flights triple in price, and some small restaurants close for a week. The upside: lantern festivals and fireworks displays in Guilin city are spectacular.</li>
    <li><strong>Labor Day (May 1–5):</strong> A 5-day spike in domestic tourism. Yangshuo gets busy but it's manageable — book hotels early.</li>
    <li><strong>Golden Week (Oct 1–7):</strong> The worst time to visit. Hotels cost 3x normal, Li River cruise tickets sell out days in advance, and every viewpoint is shoulder-to-shoulder. Avoid unless you have no other option.</li>
  </ul>

